a high-profile company is making a  personal push to end a life-shortening  disease ali leforce is a sideline  reporter for cbs sports and the turner  networks for march madness now her  husband is joe smith he is pitched in  more major league games than any other  active player their mission to help  families have babies born free of  huntington's disease that's a hereditary  illness that destroys the brain cells  cbs saturday morning co-host dana  jacobson spoke with them about their  work and their own struggles to have a  child this is such a personal story glad  they're sharing yeah it is really  amazing and they've been very open from  the outside looking in ali and joe's  life to many may seem like a fairy tale  they are certainly a sports power couple  but after joe's mom was diagnosed with  huntington's disease they made the  decision to only have kids through ivf  to make sure that they do not pass on  the disease then in the midst of their  own personal journey they decided to  focus on helping other couples as well  this is the very beginning of our  journey to make our first child together  we have three frozen embryos without  huntington's disease for over three  years  i did it turner sports sideline reporter  ali laforce has been open and candid  about her journey to have a baby  day five blood work using social media  to document the highs bye little friend  and lowe's today was one of the most  emotional  and  really difficult days  including a miscarriage it's not fair if  you only post the good and not the bad  because that's not real life i just  didn't feel right not sharing because i  don't want women to think that  something's wrong with them when there's  nothing wrong with them we're all going  through our own personal journey we all  have struggles the struggle for ally and  her husband joe to have a baby born  without huntington's disease or hd a  genetic condition that causes the  breakdown of nerve cells in the brain  joe lost his grand